             Installing Red Hat Linux 4.2
             on
             DIGITAL Personal Workstation
             a- or au-series Systems

             September 1997

             This document describes how to install Linux/Alpha from the
             Red Hat Linux 4.2 distribution on a DEC PWS a- or au-series
             Alpha workstation. The instructions describe how to set up a
             workstation for Linux/Alpha only or for both Linux/Alpha and
             Windows NT. The document assumes a knowledge of either Windows
             NT or DIGITAL UNIX, but not necessarily of Linux itself.

             Table of Contents

             Preparing for installation
                  Obtaining network information
                  Before installing Linux and Windows NT on same hard drive
             Creating installation diskettes
                  Copying and writing disk images using Windows NT
                  Copying and writing disk images using DIGITAL UNIX
             Installing Red Hat Linux/Alpha
                  Setting up AlphaBIOS
                  Booting MILO
                  Using Red Hat installation program
             Setting up Linux/Alpha system
                  Setup for automatic booting using
             AlphaBIOS
                  Setting system type
                  Building kernels for Personal
             Workstation a-series
                       Applying
             Linux/Alpha patches
                       Updating
             the built-in Ethernet adapter
             Installing other software
                  Linux/Alpha EM86
                  Netscape 3.01 for Linux/x86

             Preparing for installation

             You can install Red Hat Linux/Alpha using any one of the
             following methods:

                * From CD-ROM (ATAPI on PWS)
                * From hard disk
                * Over the network via FTP (through firewall proxies), NFS,
                  or SMB (Server Message Block): a Windows 95/NT File Share

             Obtaining network information

             If you choose to install over the network, you need to obtain
             the network  information described in Table 1 and Table 2 from
             your network administrator. Table 1 describes parameters
             pertaining to the system on which you're installing
             Linux/Alpha. Table 2 describes parameters pertaining to the
             server you're installing from.

             Table 1 Network Information common to FTP, NFS, and SMB

              Network            IP address
              information        netmask
              common to FTP,     default gateway
              NFS, and SMB...    primary/secondary/tertiary
                                 domain name servers
                                 domain name
                                 hostname
                                 network adapter (if other than
                                 built-in)

             Table 2 Network information specific to FTP, NFS, or SMB

              Network
              information
              specific to...
              FTP                FTP site name (hostname or IP
                                 address)
                                 Red Hat distribution directory
                                 username and password for
                                 non-anonymous server
                                 or proxy server (if required)
              NFS                NFS server name (hostname or IP
                                 address)
                                 Red Hat distribution directory
              SMB                server name (hostname or IP
                                 address)
                                 share volume name
                                 account name
                                 password

             Before installing Linux and Windows NT on same hard drive

             If you intend to install both Linux and  Windows NT in
             separate partitions of the same hard drive, create the NT
             partitions first. (Use the NT Install or Disk Administrator
             program.) When you then use the Red Hat installation process
             to create the Linux partitions, you will already know how much
             space has been allocated to Windows NT.

             The contents of the MILO install diskette (linload.exe and
             milo) can be copied onto the NT boot partition (of type DOS
             FAT) for automatic booting without a floppy diskette. More
             information on that can be found in Section 5.1.

             Creating installation diskettes

             Use three blank 3.5" high density floppy diskettes to create
             the following three disk images:

              Disk 1   The MILO, or Miniloader, diskette, which
                       holds software necessary to boot Linux
              Disk 2   The boot diskette, which holds kernel,
                       or core part of the operating system
              Disk 3   The root diskette, which holds
                       installation software and other
                       miscellaneous items

             Depending on which system you are using to prepare the Linux
             installation, go to:

             Windows NT instructions
             DIGITAL UNIX instructions

             Copying and writing disk images using Windows NT

             Use this section if you are using Windows NT to install Linux.
             Table 3 shows how to copy the installation disk images from
             CD-ROM, hard drive or the network. To write the disk images
             using Windows NT, you need the RAWRITE utility (rawrite.exe).
             If necessary, use Table 4 to find out how to obtain this
             utility. Table 5 shows how to write the disk images to the
             three floppy diskettes.

             All disk images are copied to a temporary local directory:
             c:\temp.

             Table 3 Copying disk images using Windows NT

              If copying from     Assuming CD-ROM is drive D,
              CD-ROM...           type:

                                  copy d:\milo\images\miata.img
                                  c:\temp\milo.img
                                  copy d:\images\miata.img
                                  c:\temp
                                  copy d:\images\ramdisk.img
                                  c:\temp
              If copying from     Assuming c:\redhat directory,
              hard drive...       type:

                                  copy
                                  c:\redhat\milo\images\miata.img
                                  c:\temp\milo.img
                                  copy c:\redhat\images\miata.img
                                  c:\temp
                                  copy
