Some weeks ago I posted about problems when making swap at
the end of a 2.1GB drive.  After several "it does work"
reports I found the reason: I didn't reboot after modifying
partition table.  That makes mkswap crash (but for some
reason mke2fs doesn't crash). (*)

At least now that I fdisk'd that drive (or two last partitions)
and rebooted, I was able to make swap at the end, with
'-c' and without, no error messages.  And to make sure
reboot really helped, I changed partition table and tried
mkswap without reboot: it crashed.  Enough proof, I think.

So no need to worry in that respect, Linux works well
with large drives.  It's just some users who aren't that
smart. ;-)


	Mika Kortelainen

(*) Both mke2fs and mkswap make filesystems, but why just
the second crashes?  Is there some real reasons for
that (swap partition has to be made with more care?)
or is there a bug in mkswap?  Both binaries are from
RH 4.9.1 (Mustang) distribution.



