I couldn't find a copy of my original post on the news server, so I had
to crib some content from e-mailed responses.  Many thanks to all who
responded.

> I came into posession of an AXPpci 33 motherboard (21066 @ 166Mhz),
> and decided to set it up for use as a Linux box.  Well, I'm in the stages
of
> just getting the hardware up, and I am a little bit stumped.  What I have
> is:
>
> AXPpci 33 w/ 21066 @ 166Mhz
> 1 MB 20ns L2 cache
> (2) 32 MB 60ns parity SIMMs
> S3 Virge PCI video card
>
> I connected up the mainboard power to a 250w P/S, connected the case
> speaker, a keyboard and a monitor.  Flip the switch, the power comes on
> and nothing.  Nada.  Bubkis.  I don't even get a video bios display on
the
> monitor.  I mucked around with some jumper settings to correct what I
> thought was an incorrect CPU setting, but still nothing.  I swapped out
> the S3 card for a basic Trident ISA VGA card.  No dice.

So I did component elimination... and with the cache removed, the 
configuration of:

AXPpci 33 w/ 21066 @ 166Mhz
(2) 32 MB 60ns parity SIMMs
S3 Virge PCI video card

At least boots up to the ARC console OK.  So the base config should
work (I've known some folks that use the S3 cards on these boards
without a hitch, so I'm hoping I will be so blessed).  Of course, I'm
not sure what kind of problems I will run into with using the 60ns
RAM (and for those of you who asked, it does appear that the RAM
is true parity - it has one additional chip per 4 MB on the SIMM).
I may try to scare up some 70ns SIMMs at a show this weekend.

So, it must be the cache, right?  Well, sort of... I thought I had been
pretty religious about carefully inserting the DIPs, but I apparently 
had two pins on one chip bent under the chip - not that I could tell
this until I removed the chip (and had removed most of the others
prior).  So I was *really* careful this time, and lo' and behold - it 
booted to the ARC console with the 1 MB of cache installed.  The 
firmware appears to see it, so I am hoping that there will be no 
further compatability woes.

So far, it looks like PEBCAC (Problem Exists Between Chair
And Computer) syndrome.  The many suggestions to solving
my problem were well thought out, and I ran a number of them
down, but it turned out to be not being quite careful enough.

Did I mention I *hate* installing DIP memory?

-- 
