: Yes shure having enough virtual memory available tends to help.
Yes, but it does not use the extra swap! It just has to exist...

: >Ah ... another point (or better two):
: > - Why has glibc grown to over twice the size it used to be?
: Could be due to extra functionallity that was added in glibc since the old ones.
Extra? Everything worked before, so what extras could be added that are such
big?

: > - Is it possible that the new glibc is slower?
: I 've really not noticed but I think your problem lays in the swap + memory 
: size on your box.
: Mine has 128 MB real memory and 128 Mb swap space normally but if I remove the 
: ram and put 32 Mb on this box it slows down to a crawl. nomally it only hits 
: the swap on accasion so I would guess that if you turned your swap space to 
: something near to the 64Mb and you use 64Mb Ram it may improve performance. 
It also depends on how you installed your memory, i.e. how many SIMMs you
use. In some cases you may only have 64 bit memory access rather than 128
bit. And this IS slower, yes. Everything else cannot slow down the system
if swap is not touched. And since mpg123 is running I do not hope that parts
are swapped out and in again ;) So swap and/or memory cannot be the point.

: Because the memory managment on the alpha seems to be quite coarse compared to 
: the intel. 
Hmm ... dunno. But what I know is that the UDB Alphas are mostly slower than
comparable other Alphas because they use smaller RAM bandwidth. For example
on a same configured Jensen and UDB the Jensen is faster though the UDB CPU
is slightly faster. This is because, so I think, the Jensen can 128 bit
memory access and the UDB only 64 bit.

