Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


> Does it significantly improve on the max number of files per process as
> compared with other Unixes? I believe 255 is a typical maximum...

You can change this by editing some header files and recompiling the
kernel.  I have mine running with 4096 per process and 8192 system-wide.
I have tested it with 4000 sockets open between two applications
(8000 file descriptors total).

I was able to change it to 2048 without difficulty, but to get the
really large numbers I had to edit another header file. 

This is under Linux 2.0.0, Intel-based PC.  With this number of 
inodes, LILO behaves strangely -- if I let it boot straight into the
modified kernel, it works just fine, but if I hit SHIFT and select
that kernel, root's console hangs when logging on (the rest of the
machine is fine).

I should be getting an Alpha soon, and will make the same changes to
it (fd limits + a few others).

Also note that with large numbers of FDs, things like select() take
quite a while, as does forking (as thousands of FDs are copied), and
exiting a program takes a while, too (as thousands of inodes are
freed).

If you want specific instructions on how to do this, e-mail me and
I will pull up my header files and write back.



