I just recently purchased a Microway Screamer (533 MHz) using the same
Motherboard
as the one you're interested in.

It works great - but Linux/Alpha is not quite as stable as Linux/Intel.
What this means is that often packages won't compile out of the box - you
need to
tinker around a bit.   Some packages this works, others it doesn't.
If you're developing your own programs, this won't impact you much.
I think most of these problems are differences in libc and glibc for the
two platforms.

For my numerical analysis programming I use gcc and have had no problems
porting a lot of code from Linux/Intel to Linux/Alpha.

Re: Fortran- From what I understand g77 doesn't perform quite as well as
DEC f77,
but I think the folks at egcs are working hard on remedying this.
The latest release (g77-0.5.21) is pretty good.  (Not the f2c converter
released
in RH4.2).

The memory used in these motherboards has to be 10 ns ECC sdram.  You'll
probably
want to get specific manufacturer part numbers (Microway supplies these).

I upgraded recently from 128 to 256 meg and it worked very nicely
(although the 64 meg
DIMM's were about $450/each).


