On Fri, 21 Nov 1997 09:18:14 +0100, Philippe Le Foll <phillf@iu-vannes.fr> posted:
>M.C. Lewis wrote:
>
>> > Does it significantly improve on the max number of files per process as
>> > compared with other Unixes? I believe 255 is a typical maximum...
>>
>
>I noticed an other limittatin to filesize that is typicaly related to 32bit.
>
>It looks like a file even of Alpha is limitted to 2GB.

This is a fairly general UNIX problem.  As UNIXes are gradually moving
towards 64 bit architectural support, this becomes an issue for all UNIX
vendors, hence the "Large File Standard Summit."

Those involved with architecting Alpha support decided *not* to create
an Alpha-specific "hack" to make Linux-Alpha support huge files until
the UNIX standards bodies come out with a common set of semantics that
can be usable on the various versions of Linux.

No sense in creating still *another* implementation of Terabyte files when
it would need to be done again probably in 1998, thus breaking any code created
for the "hack."
