>here's my question:  
>   I want to use NFS to be the root directory for some PC's.. I've already
>made a copy of the i386 linux (that works fine) on the nfs directory that
>I'll be using as root for those boxes, HOW THE CRAP DO I MAKE THE BOOT
>FLOPPY!!! lilo(running off of one of the intel boxes) is configured
>correctly I think, pointing toward nfs and the ip and subnet and all that
>good stuff of the Alpha box.  But lilo says it cannot read "/dev/fd0" and
>it won't install it's bootloader onto the floppy.. I've tried just putting
>the kernel on a floppy, but I need to load a module for my network card
>(3Com 3c905) which is not supported by any kernel internally.... argggghh
>
>
>Ron Trolard
>

There is a HOWTO that covers boot-floppies, it is not a simple thing.  You
must write the kernel directly to the floppy (without a filesystem) and then
write a compressed root drive just past the kernel (including module support)
and then tell the kernel where on the floppy to find it (using rdev).  Lotsa
fun.  You learn a lot about kernels and booting and filesystems when you work
on boot disks... 

G'luck.

Steve Borho
steve@meridian.com



