In article <01bce70e$0e947520$0201a8c0@zarathustra.site147.ping.at>,
  "Peter Sima" <psima@ping.at> wrote
> I intend to get a system using a PC164SX or PC164LX board, so my
> major question is: is the Windows NT firmware the board is equipped
> with sufficient for running Linux on it, or do I have to order a DEC UNIX
> firmware?

Absolutely. It (NT/ARC firmware) does have the drawbck of being written
by MS(I believe), but other than that it works fine.

It does like to have a small FAT partition for OS loaders, but you can
work around this if you refuse to give up 5MB of disk space.

For the installation you tell it to look for a:  \linload.exe  (assuming
that is where you put the above mentioned exe) For booting you can place
linload and milo on this small FAT partition and in the firmware setup
point to this partition and give it the boot/root param: boot
hdXX:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/hdXX	(your drive or SCSI if no IDE)

You can also blow milo into the flash, but I have not yet played with
that.

Have fun,

--Mike

