Organization: Moene Computational Physics, Maartensdijk, The Netherlands
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha,comp.lang.fortran


Hi All,

The performance of g77 generated binaries on Alpha's has been lacking, ever 
since we got it working on this architecture (g77-0.5.20).

Two people independently pointed out to me what was wrong: David 
Mosberger-Tang and Claus Denk, an Alpha-alpha :-) tester of g77.
It turned out that array index computations were carried out in plain INTEGER 
arithmetic, whereas the final address computation is done in the `mode' 
(gcc-speak for "type") of pointers, which is 64 bits on Alpha's.  Therefore, 
conversion instructions were generated in the intermediate code which 
prohibited the normal optimisations on array index calculations.

This was somewhere in late June.  I promised to look into it and actually 
discussed the matter on the gcc backend mailing list.

Unfortunately, this didn't lead to a solution.  With the creation of the EGCS 
(experimental GNU compiling system) effort, a new opportunity to take this up 
appeared.  And indeed, after a week of intermittent discussion on the EGCS 
mailing list, Richard Henderson came up with two patches (one to the g77 
front end - the Fortran specific part of g77) and one to the (general) 
backend of the compiler.  Furthermore, Jeff Law upgraded the 
`loop-optimising' part of the compiler to do an even better job.

These improvements went into the latest snapshot of egcs (see 
http://www.cygnus.com/egcs) for everyone to try out.

However, do realise that this is a snapshot, so don't use it for production 
work.

To give you a flavor of the sort of improvement one gets, I just checked with 
the CFD benchmark by Patrick Queutey 
(http://perso.club-internet.fr/queutey/Linux/Bench/bench.html, the 21x21x21 
case).  Before the change, on Greg Lindahl's 500 Mh 21164 based machine, I 
got around 10 seconds on this benchmark, now I get:

3.73user 0.01system 0:07.79elapsed 48%CPU (0avgtext+0avgdata 0maxresident)k
3.57user 0.01system 0:08.07elapsed 44%CPU (0avgtext+0avgdata 0maxresident)k
3.77user 0.01system 0:08.55elapsed 44%CPU (0avgtext+0avgdata 0maxresident)k
3.64user 0.01system 0:07.26elapsed 50%CPU (0avgtext+0avgdata 0maxresident)k

Hope this is of interest.

PS: Special thanks to Greg Lindahl to offer me access to an Alpha for looking 
into this problem !
-- 
