Hi Jon,

In the event you get no other replies, I can provide some negative but
perhaps pertinent information.

SCSI is SCSI, but the Macintosh uses a subset of the SCSI-1/SCSI-2
command set. As most Mac SCSI devices were manufactured by someone other
than Apple, this is generally not a problem, *BUT*; Macintosh computers
do not use parity on their SCSI chain.

As you have found, the early NEC CD-ROM drives are a bit screwy (I used
to think that NEC hardware was top notch, but experience does not bear
this out). If I remember correctly, the 2V was a somewhat, uh..., crude
design even by NEC standards.

I have two NECs, a CDR-74 and a CDR-210, which will not work on the UDB.
Both are single session, SCSI-1, and the CDR-210 at least does not
support parity. Both work OK with Windoze, are somewhat broken on
Linux-Intel, and the UDB doesn't like them at all. My symptoms were that
when installing RedHat the install script would appear to find the CD
drive, but then hang forever forcing a re-boot.

Before you trash the drives, take a look at the jumpers.  If possible
enable parity and disconnect-reconnect. If the 2V doesn't support parity
I think you may as well chuck it as far as the UDB is concerned. 

I am happy to report that the still available, but increasingly
inexpensive, Toshiba 3401B works fine.

Charles



