In article <63gfj1$iio@dfw-ixnews5.ix.netcom.com>,
    Stephan Schaem <t21@ix.netcom.com> wrote:
>In article <63dhp1$gl4$1@sue.cc.uregina.ca>,
>    bayko@borealis.cs.uregina.ca (John  Bayko) wrote:
[...]
>>    Hewlett-Packard sells Intel (MS Windows 95 and NT) and PA-RISC
>>(Unix, MPE/MPX) lines. IBM sells Intel (MS Windows and NT, IBM OS/2),
>>PowerPC (AIX, Microware OS-9, OS/400), S/390 (OS/390, VM/CMS) lines.
>>    There's no reason SGI can't sell more than one processor line as
>>well.
>
> So you really expect SGI to soon come out with a line of PPC box?
> My question was that SGI didn't choose x86 by chance. Merced come
> to mind, x86&windows market come also to mind.
> The PPC/alpha future is grim, and its market is minimal. No point for SGI.

    I believe that Cray, a subsidiary of SGI, still currently sells an
Alpha-based multiprocessor system (Cray T3E). However MIPS processors
are competitive with Power at every level, so there would be no need
for that.
    There is a very popular software base for Intel 80x86 processors,
specifically Windows 97. That's about the only reason for a company to
produce an Intel based line, since the processors themselves have no
real advantages over the competition.
    As for Merced, Intel has made other attempts to switch to RISC -
namely the 80860 and 80960 - which didn't even scratch the 80x86
market. Chances are that it will be more beneficial for HP than Intel,
given this track record.
    This will put the 80x86 series *two* paradigms behind state of the
art (RISC and VVLIW), and the performance gap will increase further.
However, users aren't concerned with this gap, they are only concerned
with getting faster versions of the systems which they already have,
so as long as the 80x86 *can* become faster, it will remain the
default PC architecture.
    Which is basically what you were asserting, I suppose. The problem
with that is not that it's wrong, but that PCs are not the entire
computer universe. This is where the other processor architectures are
not just important, but essential. At the high end, 80x86 processors
can't compete with MIPS, Alpha, or PowerPC. At the low end, they waste
far too much power to compete with ARM, ColdFire, or SH.
    And these are not insignificant markets - Alpha NT, and Alpha,
PowerPC, SPARC, MIPS and other Unix servers are growing rapidly, while
more and more people are getting PDAs from Psion, Pilot, and Apple.
And those PDAs are becoming more like PCs all the time - it won't be
too long before hybrid systems which can be used as desktops provide
much of the same functionality of PCs. There are also dedicated
systems like NCs and SetTop boxes, video games (you'll never see an
80x86 in one of those!).
    The upshot of this is that the non-80x86 market is not a limited
one, and is not dead by any means. Alpha, PowerPC, and all the rest
have a very strong future, independent of what happens entirely within
the PC world.

--
